00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.670 align:start position:0%
in the 1970s portugal's carnation  revolution kicked off a global wave of  democratization  as people across the world demanded  democracy from their governments  throughout the rest of the 20th century  democracy continued to grow  in popularity and legitimacy even the  most powerful autocracies like china and  russia adopted more democratic rhetoric  yet events in the 21st century have  raised concerns about democratic  backsliding  or the gradual shift away from democracy  towards authoritarianism  although the number of total elections  has been increasing  since the mid-20th century the line has  plateaued  and could be slowly declining  a new review in science evaluates  democratic backsliding at the global  level  by examining the conditions that pave  the way for backsliding  and how international support for  democracy has changed  it spotlights democracy scores based on  how well a country conducts fair  elections  protects basic freedoms and holds  elected leaders accountable  1976 marked the beginning of the most  recent wave of democratization  by 1989 countries in latin america  had transitioned from military rule to  democracies  at the turn of the 21st century the same  clustering effect occurred in eastern  europe and sub-saharan africa  but in 2019 democracy scores across the  globe went down  concerns about backsliding are  especially prominent in countries  experiencing nationalist movements  declining trust in government  institutions rising income inequality  and populism  or an ideology that pits pure people  against the self-serving elite  all pose a threat to democratic  governance  a decline in western support for  democracy has intensified these  challenges  support has taken a bigger tumble within  the last four years  than the previous 40. without  international support  citizen movements are more likely to be  suppressed by their local and national  governments  this makes it more challenging to hold  leaders accountable for acts against  democracy  such as stealing elections or limiting  individual rights  hong kong's recent pro-democracy  protests provide a unique example  in 2019 china ruled out a new national  security law that undermined hong kong's  autonomous status  the study says the international support  for hong kong was underwhelming  and it suggests that if the protests had  taken place just three years earlier  they would have met with more powerful  international support  democratic backsliding presents a  daunting reality in the years to come  you
 

